    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1832">

                <pb facs="1468" n="1352" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg1352"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 367.11/29.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                            American Ambassador</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 201.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-11-21">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 21, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I enclose herewith, for your information
                    and for the files of the Embassy, a copy of a letter<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d1832-f1"/> from the Navy Department, dated November 9, reporting
                    that, at the request of the State Department, that war vessels be sent to Smyrna
                    and Beirut, the armored cruisers <hi rendition="#italic">Tennessee</hi> and <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Montana</hi> were ordered to prepare for the cruise and
                    sail as soon as possible. The letter adds that the commander of the squadron has
                    been directed to get into communication with the Embassy and to be guided by its
                    suggestions as far as practicable.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.],</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">P. C. Knox.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
